From f6fd417ca185968826148b169d9f4c216d1ec4be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 15:49:49 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 16/18] hwmon: macsmc: Leave fan policy to firmware until a
 user requests control

The host thermal ladder assumed the SMC needed a manual request to run
the fans. Sustained macOS measurements instead show firmware ramping the
fans while the host makes no SMC key writes. Remove the autonomous fan
cooling devices and resume work; retain explicit fanX_target control and
release on suspend, shutdown and removal.

Read the mode before requesting manual control. In system mode, unlock
first and wait using reads instead of provoking a refused mode write.
Firmware without an unlock key fails without issuing a known unsupported
write.

Exercise manual targets, zero refused writes, untouched lifecycle paths,
suspend release, resume without reapplication, and unlock failures with
the existing KUnit SMC model.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
---
 Documentation/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon.rst |   5 +
 drivers/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon-test.c    | 122 ++++-------
 drivers/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon.c         | 299 ++++++---------------------
 3 files changed, 103 insertions(+), 323 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon.rst b/Documentation/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon.rst
index 2204745326cb..b0c78c341208 100644
--- a/Documentation/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon.rst
+++ b/Documentation/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon.rst
@@ -31,6 +31,11 @@ as unsafe, allows manual speeds on every fan with a mode key whatever the
 Devicetree says. It cannot be proven that the system will fail safe if it
 overheats under a manual fan speed.
 
+The driver leaves fan policy to the SMC until userspace writes a nonzero
+``fanX_target``. Thermal zones do not take fans into manual mode. Suspend,
+shutdown and removal release only fans taken over by such a request; resume
+does not reapply it. A new target write is required after resume.
+
 SoC die temperatures are described by the ``apple,soc-die-keys`` string list
 on the hwmon device node. The driver appends these temperature channels after
 the ``temperature-*`` children, in list order, with labels ``SoC die <key>``.
